 All Fives pattern? Not likely.  \n\nConsidering game history: Next player has chow W1,W2,W3; others no melds. Discards show many B and T tiles out.  \n\nI think keeping sequences is key: T5,T6 is good (need T4 or T7). B7,B8 is okay (need B6 or B9). T3 alone. Extra B5.  \nSo discard either T3 or B5. Between them, B5 is more versatile (can pair with other B5s, 1 unshown left? Actually unshown B5:1, so only one left, less chance for pung). T3 has 4 unshown, better chance for pung/pair.  \n\nThus discard B5 to keep T3 for potential pung/pair and maintain T5,T6 and B7,B8 sequences.  \n\nReason: B5 is less needed for sequences since I already have a B5 in a chow, and keeping T3 offers more potential due to more available tiles. Also, discarding B5 avoids clogging hand with middle tiles that might not form new chows easily.  \n\nAnswer: Play B5"}
